WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.780
In this video, you replicate a virtual machine's NetApp volume to a second SUSE Virtualization cluster with Trident Protect and SnapMirror, then fail the virtual machine over.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:19.560
On the retail-store cluster, the virtual machine demo-tp is running. A small service inside writes a timestamp every five seconds.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:29.020
Its only disk, demo-tp-root, is a NetApp iSCSI volume that Trident provisions on the ONTAP system.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:37.140
Trident Protect, a NetApp product, works with applications. Define the VM as one, and snapshot it every five minutes.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:46.530
After applying both, take a first snapshot right away. It captures the volume and stores the VM definition in the S3 AppVault.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:55.190
On the disaster-recovery cluster, an AppMirrorRelationship names the source application and the same AppVault, and maps the storage class.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:03.060
After the baseline transfer, the relationship is Established. The DR cluster holds a replica volume, but no virtual machine yet.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:10.680
On the DR ONTAP system, the volume is a SnapMirror destination, snapmirrored and healthy.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:17.150
For a planned failover, first disable the schedule on retail-store, so no newer snapshot overtakes the final one.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:24.130
Then take a ShutdownSnapshot. It stops the VM, takes a final snapshot and removes the VM from retail-store.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:31.880
On the disaster-recovery cluster, promote the relationship with that snapshot. Trident Protect breaks the mirror and recreates the VM.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:38.130
The summary lists how long each phase took, from the first command until the VM runs on the DR site.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:43.260
On the disaster-recovery cluster, filter the virtual machine list for demo-tp.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:50.320
demo-tp is Running here now, recreated from the AppVault on the replicated NetApp volume.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:58.780
Inside the guest, the timestamps stop when the ShutdownSnapshot stopped the VM and resume after the boot on the DR site. Nothing is missing.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:06.750
Back on retail-store, the virtual machine is gone, so only one copy runs. Its retained volume stays for a later failback.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:14.527
demo-tp now runs on the disaster-recovery cluster, with no data lost. Failback works the same way, in the other direction.
